      "program_name": "Behavioral Health Services Act (BHSA) Housing Interventions, 2026-2029 Integrated Plan",
      "administering_agency": "Nevada County Behavioral Health Department",
      "funding_type": "other",
      "typical_award_size": "Three-year BHSA plan (FY 2026/27 to 2028/29, approved June 2, 2026, Resolution 26-231) budgets $27,502,891 in BHSA spending, with the required 30% for housing interventions (about $2.75 million a year) and half of that for chronic homelessness. The County will use housing funds for rental subsidies and operating costs of its PSH units and for outreach (HOME Team, Commons Resource Center, 865 people a year); it will NOT use BHSA for capital development (0% of the allowed 25%) or for CalAIM-covered tenancy services. BHSA-funded PSH beds grew from 20 (2019) to 94 (2026) plus 24 Homekey+ units coming.",
      "per_unit_cap": "Not applicable; no capital or per-unit awards.",
      "affordability_requirement": "BHSA-eligible adults with serious mental illness or substance use disorder experiencing or at risk of homelessness; BHSA is the funding source of last resort for PSH.",
      "eligible_project_types": [
        "supportive housing"
      ],
      "application_cycle": "rolling",
      "next_deadline": "Integrated Plan runs through FY 2028/29 with annual updates; contact Nevada County Behavioral Health (530-265-1437) about operating subsidies for PSH units.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "unclear",
      "layering_notes": "Nevada County's Housing and Community Services Department (HCS, Tyler Barrington, Director, 950 Maidu Ave, Nevada City, 530-265-1645, Housing@nevadacountyca.gov) sits inside the Health and Human Services Agency and administers the Western Nevada County Regional Housing Trust Fund, PLHA (as delegated applicant for Grass Valley and Nevada City), state HOME and CDBG awards, the MORE mobile home program and the new Down Payment Assistance Program; the Behavioral Health and Homelessness Department (Cari Yardley, Director) holds Homekey+, BHSA and the Truckee Navigation Center; HHAP is administered by the Homeless Resource Council of the Sierras as the CoC CA-531 lead; and the Regional Housing Authority (Sutter, Nevada, Yuba, Colusa and Lake counties, Yuba City) supplies vouchers. Grass Valley, Nevada City and Truckee run their own programs (Pass 3). The County has no inclusionary ordinance, no housing impact fee and no affordable housing fee waiver program; the only land use incentive is the state density bonus ordinance. nevadacountyca.gov opens directly and the Legistar web API client 'nevco' exposes staff reports and attachments at legistar.granicus.com/nevco/attachments/. Nevada County's BHSA plan is operations-only, so a PSH developer should plan on BHSA as an operating or rental subsidy source for a handful of units, not as gap capital. The plan notes a large gap in permanent rental subsidies and that the Regional Housing Authority will not open or expand vouchers in 2026. Capital has come from BHBH, BHCIP, ERF and Homekey+ instead.",
